    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with a clear objective: exploit favorable matchups to gain ground in our scarcest categories. **Yordan Alvarez** (74.97) stands as our premier play, bolstered by an elite xwOBA of .452 and a favorable matchup against Avila. He is joined at the top of our confidence rankings by **Junior Caminero** (74.72) and **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (73.03). **Caminero** draws the highest-value matchup of the day against Aldegheri (5.47 xERA), while **Crow-Armstrong** remains our primary engine for stolen bases; his projected lead-off spot against Roupp offers a dynamic platoon advantage (+14%) that we anticipate will pay dividends in speed accumulation.\n\nBeyond our top-tier plays, **Caleb Durbin** (60.06) warrants attention for his utility in our SB-starved landscape. Facing Leiter, **Durbin** benefits from the pitcher\u2019s slow delivery (85% SB success rate), allowing us to project him as a strong start over higher-base-projection options. The most difficult bench decision involved **Colt Keith** (56.96), who narrowly missed the cut. While **Keith** provides solid floor reliability against Bibee, the optimizer favored the platoon-specific upside of **Casey Schmitt** (63.03) and the current form of **Luke Raley** (62.96). \n\nWe must remain vigilant regarding our high-profile leadoff hitters, as **Trea Turner** (50.39) and **Kyle Schwarber** (49.64) are both facing the formidable arm of Misiorowski (2.79 xERA). Their scores are suppressed by the opposing SP skill, making them marginal starts relative to our usual expectations. Additionally, **Christian Yelich** (47.61) carries inherent risk today due to a negative platoon split against Banks and the presence of an elite catcher behind the plate, which creates a drag on his stolen base probability. We will monitor the lineup status for **Crow-Armstrong** closely to ensure the assumed lead-off position holds before lock.",
